---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { REPO } from '../../lib/site';

// Directive defaults — the source of truth is docs/config.md.
const limits = [
  ['max_parts', '2000', 'parts in one multipart body'],
  ['max_files', '200', 'file parts in one body'],
  ['max_part_headers', '16', 'header lines in any one part (best-effort)'],
  ['max_part_header_bytes', '8192', 'bytes of any one part header (best-effort)'],
  ['max_field_name_bytes', '1024', 'bytes of a form-field name'],
  ['max_filename_bytes', '512', 'bytes of an uploaded filename'],
  ['max_parse_memory_bytes', '268435456', 'total body bytes processed (256 MiB)'],
  ['max_input_vars', '2000', 'GET+POST+cookie variables in one request'],
  ['max_input_nesting_depth', '64', 'array nesting of one name: a[b][c] = 2'],
  ['max_input_key_bytes', '1024', 'bytes of any single variable name / array key'],
  ['max_request_headers', '200', 'request headers in one request'],
  ['max_request_header_bytes', '16384', 'bytes of any one request header'],
];
const critical = ['max_parts', 'max_files', 'max_input_vars', 'max_input_nesting_depth', 'and the byte/memory limits'];
const standard = ['malformed_multipart', 'upload_progress_abuse', 'max_input_key_bytes', 'max_request_headers', 'max_request_header_bytes', 'self_test'];
// Event line fields, schema 2 — the source of truth is docs/log-schema.md
// (site/test/log-contract.test.mjs checks this list against the core).
const logFields = [
  ['schema', 'integer', '2. Lines from 1.0 to 1.4.0 have no schema key.'],
  ['version', 'string', 'Version of the extension that wrote the line.'],
  ['ts', 'string', 'UTC Unix epoch milliseconds, as a decimal string.'],
  ['request_id', 'string', 'Lowercase hex; also printed in the block response.'],
  ['layer', 'integer', '1 request-shape limits, 2 input inspection, 3 sinks, 4 after-breach behaviour.'],
  ['rule', 'string', 'Rule name, spelled as in the config.'],
  ['action', 'string', 'block or log.'],
  ['client_ip', 'string', 'REMOTE_ADDR; empty when the SAPI has none (CLI, php -S).'],
  ['method', 'string', 'HTTP method.'],
  ['uri', 'string', 'Request path; the query string is removed.'],
  ['reason', 'string', 'Human-readable detail. Match on rule, not on this text.'],
];
---
<Layout
  title="Howdah configuration & rule reference"
  description="Configure Howdah: the php.ini settings, the TOML limits file, every rule with its default and severity, and the JSON log format."
  path="/docs/configuration/"
>
  <div class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/docs/">Docs</a> / <span aria-current="page">Configuration</span></p>
      <h1>Configuration &amp; rules</h1>
      <p class="lede">Howdah reads two or three <code>php.ini</code> settings and an optional TOML file. A missing or malformed config falls back to safe defaults and never takes PHP down.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>php.ini settings</h2>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code><span class="c">; php.ini (or a conf.d/*.ini, or -d on the CLI)</span>
extension=howdah.so
howdah.config=/etc/howdah/howdah.toml   <span class="c">; path to the TOML (optional)</span>
howdah.mode=block                       <span class="c">; block | detect | off (TOML can override)</span>
howdah.log=/var/log/howdah.log          <span class="c">; event log file (wins over [log] path)</span></code></pre>
      <p>If <code>howdah.config</code> is unset, or the file is missing or unreadable, Howdah uses <code>howdah.mode</code> with the built-in limits. If the file is present but malformed, Howdah falls back to its safe defaults (block mode, the limits below) and keeps serving. That fallback is not silent: PHP's error log gets one line at startup naming the file and the parse error, and <code>php --ri howdah</code> / <code>phpinfo()</code> shows <code>Config status =&gt; INVALID (fell back to block): …</code>.</p>
      <p>To fall back to <strong>off</strong> instead, put <code>on_invalid_config = "off"</code> as a top-level line (before any <code>[table]</code>). The parser is all-or-nothing, so Howdah finds this key by scanning the raw text of a file that failed to parse; it works when a sub-table is the broken part, but a typo in that line itself means <code>block</code>. The default is <code>"block"</code>. It is a TOML key, not a php.ini directive.</p>
      <p class="note"><strong>Prefer not to hand-write the TOML?</strong> The <a href="/config/">config editor</a> builds both files from a form, explains each setting next to its control, and validates the result with the extension's own parser, so a typo cannot silently turn into block mode on your server.</p>

      <h2>The TOML file</h2>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>mode = "block"   <span class="c"># block refuses, detect logs only, off disables</span>

[limits]
<span class="c"># multipart body limits</span>
max_parts              = 2000
max_files              = 200
max_part_headers       = 16
max_part_header_bytes  = 8192
max_field_name_bytes   = 1024
max_filename_bytes     = 512
max_parse_memory_bytes = 268435456   <span class="c"># 256 MiB</span>
<span class="c"># input-shape limits</span>
max_input_vars           = 2000
max_input_nesting_depth  = 64
max_input_key_bytes      = 1024
<span class="c"># request-header limits</span>
max_request_headers      = 200
max_request_header_bytes = 16384

[log]
<span class="c"># "file" (default) | "syslog" | "stderr"; see Event log below</span>
destination = "file"
path = "/var/log/howdah/events.log"   <span class="c"># howdah.log wins when both are set</span>

[rules]
<span class="c"># override the mode for a single rule by name</span>
max_request_headers = "detect"
self_test           = "off"</code></pre>
      <p>Any limit set to <code>0</code> is rejected (<code>0</code> would block every request); there is no &ldquo;unlimited&rdquo;.</p>

      <h2>Limits and their defaults</h2>
      <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Limit defaults (scrolls sideways on narrow screens)">
        <table>
          <caption class="sr-only">[limits] directives and defaults</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Directive</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">What it bounds</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {limits.map(([k, v, d]) => (
              <tr><th scope="row">{k}</th><td>{v}</td><td>{d}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="legend">Defaults are validated by the cross-version lab matrix and the false-positive corpus (zero-block at every value above). Rationale for each is in <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/config.md`}>docs/config.md</a> and the measurements in <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/coverage-data.md`}>docs/coverage-data.md</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="layer-2-inspection">Layer 2 — input inspection (<code>[inspect]</code>)</h2>
      <p><strong>These rules only log; they do not block any request by default.</strong> Layer 2 scans decoded GET, POST, cookie and path values for SQL-injection, path-traversal and command-injection shapes. <code>inspect.mode</code> is <strong>independent</strong> of the global <code>howdah.mode</code>: a global <code>howdah.mode = block</code> does <strong>not</strong> arm Layer 2. To block, set <code>inspect.mode</code> (or one class under <code>[inspect.rule_modes]</code>) to <code>"block"</code> explicitly. Signature inspection is best-effort and can be bypassed, which is why it logs rather than blocks. Layer 2 adds no php.ini directives.</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>[inspect]
enabled   = true        <span class="c"># master on/off for Layer 2</span>
mode      = "detect"    <span class="c"># detect = log only (default); block; off</span>
sqli      = true        <span class="c"># per-class toggles</span>
traversal = true
cmdi      = true

[inspect.limits]
max_value_bytes = 16384     <span class="c"># scan at most this many bytes of one value</span>
max_total_bytes = 2097152   <span class="c"># per-request aggregate scan budget</span>
max_values      = 2000      <span class="c"># per-request value count budget</span>

[inspect.rule_modes]        <span class="c"># optional per-class override of inspect.mode</span>
<span class="c"># sql_injection = "block"   # example only; also path_traversal, command_injection</span></code></pre>
      <p class="legend">The <code>[inspect]</code> table lives in the TOML file named by <code>howdah.config</code>, not in <code>howdah.ini</code>. A <code>[inspect.rule_modes]</code> entry overrides <code>inspect.mode</code> for that class only. Values past a scan budget are not scanned. Events carry the rule, source and variable name, never the matched value. Full reference: <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/config.md`}>docs/config.md</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="layer-3-sinks">Layer 3 — sink hardening (<code>[sinks]</code>)</h2>
      <p><strong>Sinks log by default; they do not block.</strong> Layer 3 watches dangerous PHP functions and writes an event when one is called with something your allowlist does not cover. It actively guards six sinks: <code>command_exec</code> (<code>system</code>, <code>exec</code>, <code>shell_exec</code>, <code>passthru</code>, <code>popen</code>, <code>proc_open</code>), <code>unserialize</code>, <code>include</code>/<code>require</code> and <code>eval</code> on every supported PHP version (include and eval on PHP 5.x are new in 1.3), plus <code>phar</code> and <code>ssrf</code>, new in 1.3 and <strong>off by default</strong>. <code>sinks.mode</code> is <strong>independent</strong> of the global <code>howdah.mode</code>: a global <code>howdah.mode = block</code> does <strong>not</strong> arm any sink. To block, set a sink's mode to <code>"block"</code> explicitly and set its allowlist first, because with an empty allowlist every call is not allowlisted. Layer 3 adds no php.ini directives.</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>[sinks]
enabled = true            <span class="c"># master on/off for Layer 3</span>
mode    = "detect"        <span class="c"># detect = log only (default); block; off</span>

[sinks.command_exec]      <span class="c"># system/exec/shell_exec/passthru/popen/proc_open</span>
<span class="c"># mode = "block"         # optional per-sink override; set allow FIRST</span>
allow = []                <span class="c"># anything else is logged (or blocked in block mode)</span>

[sinks.unserialize]       <span class="c"># OFF by default: object unserialize is high-volume</span>
<span class="c"># mode = "detect"        # opt in (log only)</span>
allow_classes = []        <span class="c"># class names that may be unserialized</span>

[sinks.include]           <span class="c"># include/require. Inert until allow_paths is set</span>
allow_paths = []          <span class="c"># directories include/require may load from</span>

[sinks.eval]

<span class="c"># New in 1.3. Both OFF by default; armed only by an explicit per-sink mode.</span>
[sinks.phar]              <span class="c"># phar:// guard</span>
<span class="c"># mode = "detect"        # opt in (log only); "block" denies</span>
allow_paths = []          <span class="c"># realpath prefixes allowed even under block</span>

[sinks.ssrf]              <span class="c"># outbound requests to internal addresses</span>
<span class="c"># mode = "detect"        # opt in (log only); "block" denies</span>
allow_hosts = []          <span class="c"># hostname | IP | CIDR; no wildcards</span></code></pre>
      <p class="legend">The <code>[sinks]</code> table lives in the TOML file named by <code>howdah.config</code>, not in <code>howdah.ini</code>. <code>command_exec</code> <code>allow</code> patterns are anchored literal strings with an optional single trailing <code>*</code>, not regular expressions. <strong>The sink argument is never logged</strong>: events carry the sink and function name (and the class name for <code>unserialize</code>), never the command line or the serialized payload. <code>include</code> is inert until <code>allow_paths</code> is set, and blocking stops the include/eval from running. On PHP 5.x, <code>include</code> is checked at compile time, so a file served from a <strong>warm opcache</strong> is not re-inspected (<code>eval</code> is unaffected). <strong><code>[sinks.phar]</code> and <code>[sinks.ssrf]</code> are off by default and are armed only by an explicit per-sink <code>mode</code></strong>; <code>[sinks] mode</code> and <code>howdah.mode</code> do not arm them. <code>phar.allow_paths</code> is a list of realpath prefixes. On PHP 5.x/7.x the phar guard covers metadata deserialization through the stream wrapper; on PHP 8.x it is detection and denial only, with no deserialization claim, and the direct Phar API (<code>new Phar</code>, <code>PharData</code>, <code>Phar::loadPhar</code>, <code>Phar::mapPhar</code>, <code>Phar::getMetadata</code>) is not covered. <code>ssrf.allow_hosts</code> takes hostnames, IPs and CIDRs (wildcards and malformed CIDRs are rejected). SSRF is best-effort, resolve-at-check-time detection, not a connection-time boundary: DNS rebinding, redirects, <code>curl_multi_*</code>, <code>CURLOPT_RESOLVE</code>/<code>CONNECT_TO</code> and a non-string <code>CURLOPT_URL</code> bypass it, and <code>copy</code>, <code>file</code>, <code>readfile</code>, <code>SoapClient</code>, <code>DOMDocument::load*</code> and <code>simplexml_load_file</code> are not hooked. Enabling it adds a <code>getaddrinfo</code> per distinct outbound host with <strong>no timeout</strong>, so a DNS stall blocks the call. Known limitation: <code>proc_open</code> with an array command (PHP 7.4+) is not inspected. This is best-effort hardening and can be bypassed. Full reference: <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/config.md`}>docs/config.md</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="layer-4-behavior">Layer 4 — after-breach behaviour (<code>[behavior]</code>)</h2>
      <p><strong>Layer 4 logs by default; it does not block, and it does nothing until you set its lists.</strong> It watches what PHP does once something has gone wrong, such as a webshell dropped in an upload directory, through four facets: <code>deny_exec_dirs</code> (PHP run from a denied directory, as the requested script or <code>require</code>d by another script; rule <code>upload_exec</code>), <code>allow_exec_binaries</code> (an exec-family call to a program not on the list; <code>unexpected_binary</code>), <code>allow_egress_hosts</code> (an outbound connection to a host not on the list; <code>unexpected_egress</code>) and <code>deny_php_writes</code> (a PHP file written into a denied directory or from a script under one; <code>php_write</code>). <code>behavior.mode</code> is <strong>independent</strong> of the global <code>howdah.mode</code>: <code>howdah.mode = block</code> does <strong>not</strong> arm it. Set the lists before you switch to <code>block</code>. Layer 4 adds no php.ini directives. Events name the script that made the call.</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>[behavior]
enabled = true              <span class="c"># master on/off for Layer 4; false -> off regardless of mode</span>
mode    = "detect"          <span class="c"># "off" | "detect" | "block". Independent of howdah.mode</span>
observe = false             <span class="c"># true -> log would-be findings as `proposed`; never blocks</span>

deny_exec_dirs      = []    <span class="c"># e.g. ["/var/www/uploads", "/var/www/storage", "/tmp"]</span>
allow_exec_binaries = []    <span class="c"># e.g. ["/usr/bin/gs", "convert"]  (empty = none expected)</span>
allow_egress_hosts  = []    <span class="c"># e.g. ["api.stripe.com"]  (empty under detect = log all egress)</span>
deny_php_writes     = false <span class="c"># true -> flag a *.php/.phar/.phtml write into a denied dir or from a script under one</span></code></pre>
      <p class="legend"><strong><code>observe = true</code></strong> logs the binary run, the host contacted or the directory PHP ran from as a <code>proposed</code> line, to help you build the allowlists. It never blocks and never edits your config; review every proposal, because on an already-breached box the observed set can include attacker behaviour. <strong>Honest limits.</strong> Howdah only sees what PHP itself does: a detached or forked process, or a dropper that never ran through PHP, is invisible. For <code>deny_exec_dirs</code>, the requested-script case does not depend on opcache, but the <code>require</code>d-file case is checked at first compile and is best-effort (PHP 7.0+: operand string only), so a file served from a warm opcache may not be re-checked; a relative-path <code>require</code> is not checked at include time on any version. Allowlists are best-effort and can be bypassed (binary matching is by path or basename; egress shares the Layer 3 <code>ssrf</code> limits). <code>deny_php_writes</code> is scoped by origin and can still flag frameworks that compile templates into a denied directory, so scope <code>deny_exec_dirs</code> before you use <code>block</code>; it matches by file extension. Verified on PHP 5.4, 5.6, 7.4, 8.3, 8.4 and 8.5. On PHP 7.0+ Howdah installs a user opcode handler (for include/eval) at MINIT, which turns OPcache JIT off; this is unchanged since 1.3 and independent of Layer 4. Full reference: <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/config.md`}>docs/config.md</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="exceptions">Scoped exceptions (<code>[[exceptions]]</code>)</h2>
      <p>An exception turns <strong>one rule off for one scope</strong>: the executing script (absolute path, glob, or SHA-256 of the file), a request parameter (exact or prefix), a URI prefix, a client network (IPv4/IPv6 CIDR), or any combination; the facets an entry sets are ANDed, unset facets match anything, the first matching entry wins. Every entry needs a <code>reason</code>: it is the decision record the <a href="/docs/compliance/#decision-record">compliance page</a> asks for. <code>rule</code> is a rule id as spelled in the log, a layer (<code>limits</code>, <code>inspect</code>, <code>sinks</code>, <code>behavior</code>) or <code>layer.rule</code>.</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>exceptions_log = false   <span class="c"># optional: true logs every match as action "excepted"</span>

[[exceptions]]
rule   = "sinks.command_exec"
script = "/var/www/html/cron.php"      <span class="c"># the file that contains the call, as PHP reports it (__FILE__)</span>
reason = "DECISION 2026-10-24 jdoe: cron.php shells out to the mailer with per-run temp paths."

[[exceptions]]
rule       = "inspect.sql_injection"
param      = "content"                 <span class="c"># request variable name; or param_prefix = "jform["</span>
uri_prefix = "/wp-admin/"
reason     = "Editors paste SQL into posts; Layer 2 is detect-only here."

[[exceptions]]
rule        = "max_request_headers"
client_cidr = "10.0.0.0/8"             <span class="c"># REMOTE_ADDR as the SAPI reports it</span>
reason      = "The internal SSO proxy adds ~40 headers."</code></pre>
      <p class="legend"><strong>Safety rails.</strong> Critical Layer 1 rules (the multipart and input-flood limits) take no exception; an entry naming one makes the whole file invalid, loudly, through the <code>on_invalid_config</code> path. Layer 1 Standard rules take only <code>uri_prefix</code> and <code>client_cidr</code>; Layer 2 takes <code>param</code>/<code>param_prefix</code>/<code>uri_prefix</code>/<code>client_cidr</code> but no <code>script</code> (its events are raised while PHP registers request variables, before any script runs); Layers 3 and 4 take <code>script</code>/<code>script_sha256</code>/<code>uri_prefix</code>/<code>client_cidr</code>. An exception is decided after the rule found something and before the mode applies: it never arms anything and never widens an allowlist. <strong>An exception on <code>sinks.command_exec</code> means that sink is not checked for that script.</strong> The <code>script</code> facet is compared with what PHP reports for the executing file (the file that holds the call, a <code>require</code>d file or a framework's filesystem class, not the page requested; symlinks resolved by PHP; matched byte for byte). <code>php --ri howdah</code> shows <code>Exceptions ([[exceptions]]) =&gt; N configured, M rejected</code>; the SAPI error log gets one summary line per process. <strong>Honest limits:</strong> <code>client_cidr</code> is <code>REMOTE_ADDR</code> (the proxy's address behind a proxy; and under the CLI built-in server the client is not known when Layer 1 and 2 events fire); <code>uri_prefix</code> is a raw byte prefix (not decoded, not case-folded); a GET value is also seen by the path scan as <code>REQUEST_URI</code>. Verified by <code>lab/integration/exceptions.sh</code> (19 checks) on PHP 5.6, 7.4 and 8.3. The method, from a log line to an entry: <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/exceptions.md`}>docs/exceptions.md</a>; starting points for WordPress, Drupal 7, Magento 1 and 2, Joomla and PrestaShop 1.6: <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/profiles`}>profiles/</a> (also served at <a href="/profiles/wordpress.toml">/profiles/&lt;app&gt;.toml</a>) and the <a href="/config/">config editor</a>'s "Application profile" selector.</p>

      <h2>Rule modes and severity</h2>
      <p>Each <code>[rules]</code> key is a rule name set to <code>block</code>, <code>detect</code>, or <code>off</code>. Rules carry a severity:</p>
      <div class="cols two">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Critical</h3>
          <p>{critical.join(', ')}. These block <strong>even when the global mode is <code>detect</code></strong> — a detect-mode deployment still refuses the unambiguous resource-exhaustion shapes. Set a rule to <code>off</code> to fully disable it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Standard</h3>
          <p>{standard.join(', ')}. These log in <code>detect</code> and block only in <code>block</code> mode — the shapes an unusual-but-legitimate client can hit.</p>
          <p>Layer 2 adds <code>sql_injection</code>, <code>path_traversal</code> and <code>command_injection</code>, also severity Standard but <strong>log-only</strong>: they are governed by <code>inspect.mode</code>, not the global mode, so <code>mode = "block"</code> does not make them block.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="legend">A few byte-limit <strong>rule</strong> names end <code>_len</code> where the matching <code>[limits]</code> <strong>directive</strong> ends <code>_bytes</code> (for example the rule <code>max_filename_len</code> for the directive <code>max_filename_bytes</code>).</p>

      <div class="note">
        <h3>Enforcement timing (honest caveat)</h3>
        <p>The <strong>multipart limits</strong> refuse the request while the body is parsed, before PHP allocates per-part memory, and emit a 403/413 before the script runs.</p>
        <p>The <strong>input-shape and request-header limits</strong> work on the request-variable and <code>$_SERVER</code> stream: Howdah <strong>drops</strong> the offending variables (bounding the symbol table — the real DoS mitigation) and writes the event, but the 403 response is best-effort and may be emitted at request shutdown, after the script produced output, on SAPIs that populate superglobals lazily (<code>auto_globals_jit=On</code>). Request-header counting depends on the app (or PHP) materializing <code>$_SERVER</code>. Treat the var-drop + log as the guarantee and the 403 as best-effort.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="log">Event log (<code>[log]</code>)</h2>
      <p>Howdah writes one JSON line per event: to a file (the default), to syslog, or to stderr. Each line carries the schema version, the Howdah version, a timestamp (<code>ts</code>, UTC epoch milliseconds), the request ID, the layer, the rule, the action, the client IP, the method, the path and the reason. The request ID also appears in the block response, so a user report maps to a log line. These three lines are from a lab run (PHP 7.4, php-fpm): a field flood refused by <code>max_parts</code>, a Layer 2 SQL-injection shape, and a Layer 3 <code>system()</code> call that is not on the allowlist.</p>
      <pre tabindex="0" data-sample="event"><code>{`{"action":"block","client_ip":"203.0.113.7","layer":1,"method":"POST","reason":"multipart part count exceeds limit","request_id":"11eccf6d90ad2987d","rule":"max_parts","schema":2,"ts":"1792864974141","uri":"/index.php","version":"1.4.0"}
{"action":"log","client_ip":"203.0.113.7","layer":2,"method":"GET","reason":"sql_injection pattern in get variable 'q'","request_id":"1a2d239820e2f06bc","rule":"sql_injection","schema":2,"ts":"1792864974146","uri":"/index.php","version":"1.4.0"}
{"action":"log","client_ip":"203.0.113.7","layer":3,"method":"GET","reason":"non-allowlisted command via system","request_id":"1fa7c36ea3c170856","rule":"command_exec","schema":2,"ts":"1792864974151","uri":"/index.php","version":"1.4.0"}`}</code></pre>
      <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Event log fields (scrolls sideways on narrow screens)">
        <table>
          <caption class="sr-only">Fields of an event line, schema 2</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Content</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {logFields.map(([f, t, d]) => <tr><th scope="row"><code>{f}</code></th><td>{t}</td><td>{d}</td></tr>)}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>The eight fields from 1.x keep their names and types; <code>schema</code>, <code>version</code> and <code>layer</code> are new in schema 2. Adding a field does not change <code>schema</code>; removing, renaming or retyping one does. The full contract, with the rule-to-layer table, is <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/log-schema.md`}>docs/log-schema.md</a>, and <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/howdah-event.schema.json`}>howdah-event.schema.json</a> is its JSON Schema.</p>
      <p class="legend">Request bodies, cookies, authorization headers, query strings, matched values and sink arguments are never logged (privacy / GDPR). Retention and a blocked-attack summary: <a href="/docs/compliance/#audit-log">compliance evidence</a>.</p>

      <h3>Where lines go</h3>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`[log]
destination = "file"            # "file" (default) | "syslog" | "stderr"
path = "/var/log/howdah/events.log"   # for "file"; the howdah.log ini value wins
syslog_facility = "local0"      # for "syslog"
syslog_ident = "howdah"         # for "syslog": the tag
# exec = "/usr/local/sbin/howdah-alert"   # off unless set
# exec_rate_limit = 10          # hook starts per second per PHP process, 1-100`}</code></pre>
      <p><strong>File</strong> (the default). The path is <code>howdah.log</code> if it is set, else <code>[log] path</code>, else stderr. Howdah opens the file for each line and writes the line in one append, so lines from concurrent workers do not interleave, and rotation needs no reload: logrotate's default rename-and-create works, and so does <code>copytruncate</code>. The PHP worker user writes the file; if it does not exist, the worker creates it with mode 0640 minus the umask, so it is not world-readable. Create it first to choose the owner, group and mode. If the file cannot be written the line goes to stderr, which php-fpm discards unless the pool sets <code>catch_workers_output = yes</code>.</p>
      <p><strong>Syslog.</strong> Each line goes to <code>/dev/log</code> (journald or rsyslog) as the message, tagged <code>howdah[pid]</code>, with severity warning for <code>block</code> and notice for <code>log</code>. <code>journalctl -t howdah -o cat</code> prints one JSON line per event. Both paths are ignored. If there is no syslog socket (a container without one, for example), or the daemon is not keeping up, the line goes to stderr with a note; the request never waits. Howdah writes to the socket itself instead of calling <code>openlog()</code>, because PHP shares and resets that state, and uses a datagram socket only: a syslog-ng set up with a stream socket at <code>/dev/log</code> gets stderr instead.</p>
      <p><strong>Stderr.</strong> Lines prefixed <code>howdah-event </code>, for containers that collect stderr.</p>
      <p><code>php --ri howdah</code> shows the result in its <code>Log destination</code> row, for example <code>syslog (/dev/log, facility local0, ident howdah)</code>.</p>

      <h3>A program per event (<code>exec</code>)</h3>
      <p>Off unless set. For every event Howdah starts the program and does not wait for it. The JSON line arrives on stdin. The environment holds only <code>HOWDAH_RULE</code>, <code>HOWDAH_ACTION</code> and a fixed <code>PATH</code>; there are no arguments, and none of the PHP worker's other open files or sockets are passed on. It is double-forked, so it is never a child (or a zombie) of the PHP worker. At most <code>exec_rate_limit</code> starts per second per PHP process (default 10); beyond that the hook is skipped and the event is still logged. On every event Howdah checks the program before it runs it: not a symlink, a regular executable file, not group- or world-writable, owned by root or the PHP user, in a directory that is not group- or world-writable. It then runs the file it checked, through <code>/proc/self/fd</code>, so swapping the file in between does not help. Use it to raise an alert, for example a chat webhook:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`#!/bin/sh
# /usr/local/sbin/howdah-alert (root:root, mode 0755)
[ "$HOWDAH_ACTION" = block ] || exit 0
exec curl -fsS -m 5 -H 'Content-Type: application/json' --data-binary @- https://hooks.example.com/howdah`}</code></pre>
      <div class="note">
        <h3>Security caveat</h3>
        <p>The program runs as the PHP worker user, whenever an attacker trips a rule. Keep it quick, owned by root and not writable by the PHP user (Howdah refuses a group- or world-writable one, but accepts one the PHP user owns, which a compromised application could rewrite). Treat its input as untrusted: the path and the names in <code>reason</code> come from the request. Starting a process from PHP is safe on the single-threaded (NTS) SAPIs Howdah supports, php-fpm, Apache mod_php with prefork, CGI and the CLI; <code>lab/integration/log_contract.sh</code> tests the built-in server, php-fpm and Apache prefork on PHP 7.4 and 8.3. Threaded (ZTS) PHP is not supported by Howdah at all.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 id="siem">Ship to a SIEM</h3>
      <p>The line is plain JSON, so any collector that reads a JSON-lines file or syslog can take it. Two starting points, both run against real event lines in the real tools by <code>lab/integration/siem_snippets.sh</code>:</p>
      <p><strong>Wazuh</strong> (manager 4.12): read the file with <code>&lt;log_format&gt;json&lt;/log_format&gt;</code> on the agent, or let syslog carry the lines and add the decoder. The <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/docs/integrations/wazuh`}>rules</a> raise level 10 for <code>block</code> and level 5 for <code>log</code>.</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`<decoder name="howdah">
  <program_name>^howdah</program_name>
  <plugin_decoder>JSON_Decoder</plugin_decoder>
</decoder>`}</code></pre>
      <p><strong>Loki</strong> via Promtail 3.5: <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/integrations/promtail-howdah.yaml`}>the pipeline</a> takes the entry time from <code>ts</code> and labels <code>rule</code>, <code>action</code> and <code>layer</code> (not <code>client_ip</code> or <code>request_id</code>, which have too many values for labels).</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`    pipeline_stages:
      - json:
          expressions:
            ts: ts
            rule: rule
            action: action
            layer: layer
      - labels:
          rule:
          action:
          layer:
      # ts is UTC epoch milliseconds, as a string.
      - timestamp:
          source: ts
          format: UnixMs`}</code></pre>
      <p class="legend">Not tested: other SIEMs, Promtail's journal and syslog targets, and Grafana Alloy. Howdah itself sends nothing over the network.</p>
    </div>
